---
import '../../styles/global.css';
import model from '../../../model/dynamos-v2.json';

const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const base = rawBase.endsWith('/') ? rawBase : `${rawBase}/`;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Frequently asked questions about DYNAMOS: what it measures, how it was developed, how PRECEPTA works, how it relates to diagnostic constructs, and what it does not do."
    />
    <title>FAQ — DYNAMOS</title>
  </head>

  <body>
    <header class="site-header">
      <a class="wordmark" href={base}>DYNAMOS</a>
      <nav aria-label="Primary navigation">
        <a href={`${base}model/`}>Model</a>
        <a href={`${base}precepta/`}>PRECEPTA</a>
        <a href={`${base}explore/`}>Explore</a>
        <a href={`${base}research/`}>Research</a>
        <a aria-current="page" href={`${base}faq/`}>FAQ</a>
        <a href={`${base}responsible-use/`}>Responsible use</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="faq-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">Start here</p>
        <h1>Understanding DYNAMOS</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          DYNAMOS can look complicated because it begins below the level of familiar labels.
          This page explains why, how the model was built, what PRECEPTA adds, what the research
          is testing, and why a DYNAMOS profile is not a diagnosis.
        </p>

        <div class="faq-core">
          <p>
            <strong>The shortest explanation:</strong> DYNAMOS does not assume that a diagnostic
            label is a mechanism. It asks which operating mechanisms can produce the phenomena
            that diagnostic labels describe.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="faq-map" aria-label="FAQ topics">
        <a href="#basics">What is it?</a>
        <a href="#origin">How was it developed?</a>
        <a href="#precepta">What is PRECEPTA?</a>
        <a href="#diagnosis">What about diagnosis?</a>
        <a href="#research">What is the evidence?</a>
        <a href="#use">How should it be used?</a>
      </section>

      <section id="basics" class="faq-section">
        <div class="faq-section-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">01 · The basics</p>
          <h2>What DYNAMOS is trying to describe</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-list">
          <details open>
            <summary>What is DYNAMOS?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                DYNAMOS — the Dynamic Neuro-Operational Systems Model — is a non-diagnostic
                systems-architecture framework. It describes how a human system operates under
                meaning, load, interaction, context, and time.
              </p>
              <p>
                Instead of asking whether someone resembles a predefined category, DYNAMOS asks
                questions such as: How easy is it to enter a task state? How robustly is that state
                maintained? What interrupts it? What does re-entry cost? How much social, cognitive,
                sensory, or affective coupling can the system sustain? What changes with context?
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Is DYNAMOS a personality test?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                No. It is not intended to sort people into stable personality types. The dimensions
                describe operating tendencies and costs that may change in expression across context,
                load, relationships, and time.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Why 26 dimensions?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                The current model separates mechanisms that are often collapsed together. Starting a
                task, sustaining it, being knocked out of it, and rebuilding the state after interruption
                are not assumed to be the same process. Social, cognitive, sensory, and affective coupling
                costs are also separated rather than treated as one general “difficulty.”
              </p>
              <p>
                The 26-dimension version emerged from repeated model expansion when existing dimensions
                could not adequately explain phenomena found in the literature without conflating distinct
                mechanisms.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>What do the 1–10 scores mean?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                They are self-ratings of your typical functioning over time. For each dimension,
                the low and high ends describe the direction of that dimension; you choose the number
                that best fits your usual experience.
              </p>
              <p>
                The scores are not population percentiles, clinical thresholds, or statements of ability
                or worth. DYNAMOS does not currently have population norms. Once enough research data
                exist, empirical distributions may support calibration in a future version; the current
                pilot does not make that claim.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="origin" class="faq-section">
        <div class="faq-section-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">02 · Where it came from</p>
          <h2>From surface descriptions to operating mechanisms</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-list">
          <details open>
            <summary>How did you come up with DYNAMOS?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                The starting point was not “invent 26 traits.” The starting point was the existing
                research literature describing recurring experiences and observed patterns associated
                with autism, ADHD, giftedness, masking, attentional regulation, sensory processing,
                cognitive load, recovery, and related areas.
              </p>
              <p>
                Instead of accepting those labels as the causal explanation, the development process
                treated the reported phenomena as <em>phenomenological input</em>: things that needed to
                be explained. The recurring question was:
              </p>
              <blockquote>What more elementary system dynamics could produce this pattern?</blockquote>
              <p>
                When one mechanism could not adequately explain distinct phenomena, the model was split
                or extended. That process led from an earlier, lower-resolution architecture to the
                current 26-dimensional model.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>So did DYNAMOS start from diagnostic language?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                In part, yes — but as a source of observations, not as the final explanatory structure.
                Research on autism, ADHD, giftedness, camouflaging, attention, cognitive load, and other
                areas supplied many of the phenomena that the model had to be able to represent.
              </p>
              <p>The development move was deliberately downward:</p>
              <div class="concept-flow" aria-label="Development flow">
                <span>Observed or reported phenomena</span>
                <b>↓</b>
                <span>Possible operating mechanisms</span>
                <b>↓</b>
                <span>DYNAMOS dimensions</span>
                <b>↓</b>
                <span>Combinations and PRECEPTA patterns</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>What research traditions sit underneath the model?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                The theoretical foundation is deliberately plural rather than tied to one diagnostic
                theory. Important strands include dynamic systems approaches to cognition and behavior,
                cognitive load and compensatory control, work on masking and hidden performance cost,
                idiographic approaches that distinguish within-person from between-person variation,
                neurodiversity-informed work on deficit framing and environmental fit, and relational
                accounts in which some difficulties emerge between systems rather than residing wholly
                inside one person.
              </p>
              <p>
                DYNAMOS is therefore best understood as a synthesis and model-building programme rather
                than as a renamed version of any one existing test.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Why avoid stigmatizing or deficit-first wording?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Because a surface description such as “poor attention,” “rigidity,” or “social deficit”
                can hide several different mechanisms and can mix capability with cost. DYNAMOS tries to
                describe what the system is doing before deciding whether that operation is helpful,
                costly, sustainable, or mismatched to the environment.
              </p>
              <p>
                This does not mean that difficulty, disability, or impairment are unreal. It means the
                framework does not assume that deficit language is itself an explanation.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="precepta" class="faq-section">
        <div class="faq-section-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">03 · From dimensions to a recognizable person</p>
          <h2>Why PRECEPTA exists</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-list">
          <details open>
            <summary>Why not just show the 26 scores?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Because people do not experience themselves as 26 independent measurements. The dimensions
                are the lower-level architecture. PRECEPTA is the interface layer that asks what recognizable
                patterns may emerge when several dimensions operate together.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>What is a PRECEPTA pattern?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                A PRECEPTA pattern is a higher-order description generated from combinations of dimensions.
                Examples include Flow Diver, Meaning-Gated Performer, Social Cue Load, Precision Resolver,
                Affective Decoupler, and Sensory-Seeking Stabilizer.
              </p>
              <p>
                PRECEPTA patterns are not identities or diagnoses. They are candidate descriptions of how
                an architecture may become visible in ordinary life.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Can one behavior come from different architectures?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Yes. This is one of the most important ideas in DYNAMOS. Similar outward behavior can arise
                from different internal mechanisms. Difficulty switching tasks, for example, might reflect
                state friction, strong mode integrity, costly re-entry, high maintenance robustness, overload,
                or a combination of these.
              </p>
              <p>
                This is why DYNAMOS does not infer a mechanism from one visible behavior alone.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Why are some PRECEPTA patterns marked “context-sensitive”?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Because the 26 static self-ratings can sometimes identify a compatible architecture without
                directly observing the defining context or transition. For example, the model may see the
                architecture that would support meaning-gated performance while still lacking a direct
                observation that task entry changes specifically when meaning changes.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Why are some patterns not inferred automatically at all?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Because the model should not claim to know what it did not measure. Some phenomena — such as
                insight arriving before explanation, deadline-triggered activation, repetitive sensory
                regulation, or subjective time-estimation problems — require information that the static
                26-item questionnaire does not directly provide. Those patterns can be investigated through
                optional follow-up questions rather than silently inferred.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="diagnosis" class="faq-section diagnosis-section">
        <div class="faq-section-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">04 · Diagnosis and clinical questions</p>
          <h2>Can we go back from architecture to diagnostic ideas?</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-list">
          <details open>
            <summary>Does DYNAMOS diagnose autism, ADHD, or any other condition?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                <strong>No.</strong> DYNAMOS is not a diagnostic instrument and does not produce a clinical
                threshold, likelihood, risk score, or probability of having a condition.
              </p>
              <p>
                A formal diagnosis requires a qualified healthcare professional using an appropriate clinical
                assessment process. DYNAMOS should not replace that process and should not be used to make
                medical, legal, employment, education, or accommodation decisions.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>But is there a way back from DYNAMOS to familiar diagnostic constructs?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                There is a legitimate research question, but not a current individual inference rule.
                DYNAMOS was built partly by decomposing phenomena described in diagnostic and neurodiversity
                literatures into lower-level mechanisms. In principle, research can now ask the inverse question:
              </p>
              <blockquote>
                Which regions of DYNAMOS architecture are more common among people who meet established
                diagnostic criteria, and which architectures occur across several diagnostic groups?
              </blockquote>
              <p>
                The expected relationship is many-to-many, not one-to-one. People with the same diagnosis may
                have quite different DYNAMOS architectures, while similar DYNAMOS mechanisms may occur in people
                with different diagnoses or no diagnosis.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Could DYNAMOS eventually say “this profile looks autistic” or “this looks like ADHD”?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Not responsibly on the evidence currently available. Doing that would require independent
                clinical ground truth, sufficiently large and appropriate samples, prospective validation,
                calibration, sensitivity/specificity analysis, replication, and careful study of comorbidity,
                age, culture, masking, referral bias, and context.
              </p>
              <p>
                The more interesting scientific use may be different: to investigate whether familiar diagnostic
                categories contain several distinct operating architectures, and whether some architectures cut
                across category boundaries.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>How does DYNAMOS relate to existing autism and ADHD questionnaires?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                There is <strong>content overlap</strong>, but there is currently no validated score conversion
                between DYNAMOS and any established instrument.
              </p>

              <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
                <table>
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Instrument</th>
                      <th>What it is designed to address</th>
                      <th>Relationship to DYNAMOS</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td><strong>ASRS</strong></td>
                      <td>Adult ADHD screening</td>
                      <td>Some attention and activation territory overlaps conceptually with DYNAMOS dimensions such as entry, maintenance, interruption, re-entry, gating, and state friction.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td><strong>RAADS-R</strong></td>
                      <td>Autism-related adult self-report used as an aid in assessment contexts</td>
                      <td>Some social, sensory, and restricted-interest territory has DYNAMOS analogs, but DYNAMOS is not a score translation or autism screener.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td><strong>CAT-Q</strong></td>
                      <td>Camouflaging strategies</td>
                      <td>Conceptually close to DYNAMOS where outward performance and hidden internal cost are separated, but CAT-Q measures a specific construct and DYNAMOS generalizes the cost/capacity distinction more broadly.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>

              <p class="faq-note">
                Content overlap is not score overlap. Two instruments can cover related territory while
                producing very different measurements.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>I think I may need an autism or ADHD assessment. Where should I start?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Start with a qualified healthcare professional who can advise on the appropriate diagnostic
                pathway in your country or health system. A primary-care clinician can often help with referral;
                specialist assessment may involve psychiatry, psychology, or another appropriately qualified
                clinical service depending on jurisdiction.
              </p>
              <p>
                If you want a preliminary self-screen before speaking with a clinician, commonly used tools
                include the <strong>WHO Adult ADHD Self-Report Scale (ASRS)</strong> for adult ADHD screening.
                In autism-related contexts you may encounter the <strong>RAADS-R</strong>; the
                <strong>CAT-Q</strong> is specifically about camouflaging rather than diagnosis.
              </p>
              <p>
                These questionnaires should not be treated as standalone diagnoses. A qualified clinician needs
                to consider developmental history, current functioning, alternative explanations, co-occurring
                conditions, context, and the relevant diagnostic criteria.
              </p>
              <p>
                DYNAMOS can be useful alongside that process because it may help you describe your lived
                operating patterns in neutral language. It cannot tell you whether you meet diagnostic criteria.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Does a DYNAMOS pattern mean I am neurodivergent?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                No. DYNAMOS dimensions and PRECEPTA patterns are intended to describe human operating
                architecture across people. A pattern may occur in diagnosed, undiagnosed, self-identified,
                or conventionally described populations. The model does not use a PRECEPTA pattern as proof
                of diagnostic or identity status.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="research" class="faq-section">
        <div class="faq-section-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">05 · Evidence and research</p>
          <h2>What we know, what we do not know, and what we are testing</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-list">
          <details open>
            <summary>Is DYNAMOS scientifically validated?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Not yet as a psychometric instrument. The current framework is theory-driven and
                literature-anchored, but it does not yet have population norms, established factor
                structure, test–retest estimates, diagnostic sensitivity/specificity, or a peer-reviewed
                validation study demonstrating that its questionnaire behaves as claimed across populations.
              </p>
              <p>
                That distinction matters: a strong theoretical basis is not the same thing as empirical
                validation of a measurement instrument.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Then what is all the underlying research doing?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                The literature provides theoretical grounding, candidate mechanisms, known phenomena, and
                constraints on model design. It helps answer whether a dimension or distinction is plausible
                and whether it corresponds to something repeatedly observed in prior research.
              </p>
              <p>
                The new DYNAMOS research programme asks a different question: when people provide their own
                26-dimensional profile, do the higher-order PRECEPTA interpretations generated from that profile
                actually describe them? And when they do not, where does the derivation fail?
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>What would proper validation look like?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Several different studies are needed rather than one headline number. The planned agenda includes
                reliability and state-sensitivity work; convergent and divergent validity against established
                instruments; testing whether dimensions cluster or remain meaningfully distinct; longitudinal work;
                and relational or dyadic studies where DYNAMOS makes claims standard individual-level tools do not.
              </p>
              <p>
                For diagnostic-adjacent questions, the key study would administer DYNAMOS alongside established
                measures and independently determined clinical outcomes. The goal would not be to force DYNAMOS
                to reproduce a diagnosis, but to map where the architectures align, diverge, or reveal multiple
                mechanisms underneath the same category.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>What does donating my data actually test?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Your 26 responses are the input. The scientifically interesting step is what happens next:
                DYNAMOS derives PRECEPTA patterns that you did not directly select, and you tell us whether you
                recognize them. Rejection and missing-pattern reports are therefore just as valuable as agreement.
              </p>
              <p>
                The public research dashboard reports aggregate model-development evidence. It should not be
                read as diagnostic validity.
              </p>
              <p><a class="text-link" href={`${base}research/`}>See the live research status →</a></p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Why collect disagreement instead of only successful matches?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Because a model that only records confirmation cannot improve. A rejected inferred pattern is a
                possible false positive. A pattern reported as missing is a possible false negative. A targeted
                follow-up may then show whether the problem is a derivation-rule gap or a measurement gap in the
                26-item questionnaire.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="use" class="faq-section">
        <div class="faq-section-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">06 · Using DYNAMOS</p>
          <h2>What the framework is for</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-list">
          <details open>
            <summary>What is DYNAMOS useful for today?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Its current intended uses are reflection, communication, education, research, and
                system-aware design. It can help people describe why apparently simple tasks may differ
                in cost, why the same person works differently in different contexts, or why two capable
                people can create high coupling load for one another.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>What should DYNAMOS never be used for?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                It should not be used as the sole or determinative basis for diagnosis, treatment, hiring,
                dismissal, promotion, grading, selection, exclusion, legal decisions, accommodations, access
                to services, or judgments about competence, suitability, productivity, reliability, or worth.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>What if my result does not feel right?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Say so. The result is a model hypothesis about your architecture, not an authority over your
                experience. The research interface is designed specifically to capture partial recognition,
                rejection, uncertainty, and important missing patterns.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>How should I cite DYNAMOS?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>For the current archived release, cite:</p>
              <blockquote>
                Berkling, K. (2026). DYNAMOS: Dynamic Neuro-Operational Systems Model (Version 2.0.0). Zenodo.
                <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22977734">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22977734</a>
              </blockquote>
              <p>
                The DOI <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22977734">10.5281/zenodo.22977734</a> identifies the exact
                v2.0.0 release. The project DOI
                <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22977733">10.5281/zenodo.22977733</a> represents DYNAMOS across
                versions and follows the evolving record.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Where should I go next on the site?</summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <div class="next-grid">
                <a href={`${base}model/`}><strong>Model</strong><span>Explore the 26 dimensions.</span></a>
                <a href={`${base}precepta/`}><strong>PRECEPTA</strong><span>Explore the higher-order patterns.</span></a>
                <a href={`${base}explore/`}><strong>Explore</strong><span>Generate your own profile locally.</span></a>
                <a href={`${base}research/`}><strong>Research</strong><span>See the live evidence programme.</span></a>
                <a href={`${base}responsible-use/`}><strong>Responsible use</strong><span>Read the boundaries and safeguards.</span></a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="faq-final">
        <p class="eyebrow">One sentence</p>
        <blockquote>
          DYNAMOS does not test whether you match a diagnostic category; it maps how your system
          appears to operate, then asks whether the higher-order interpretation actually describes you.
        </blockquote>
        <div class="faq-final-links">
          <a class="primary-link" href={`${base}explore/`}>Explore your architecture →</a>
          <a class="text-link" href={`${base}responsible-use/`}>Responsible use →</a>
        </div>
      </section>
